# Hill–Clohessy–Wiltshire Relative Motion: Visualisation

```elixir
# See hcw_orbital_mechanics.livemd's setup cell for why the project path
# is taken from the environment before falling back to __DIR__.
project_path = System.get_env("PLUMBLINE_PROJECT_PATH") || Path.join(__DIR__, "..")

Mix.install([
  {:plumbline, path: project_path},
  {:vega_lite, "~> 0.1"},
  {:kino, "~> 0.14"},
  {:kino_vega_lite, "~> 0.1"}
])
```

## What this document is

This notebook **draws** relative orbits. It does not judge them.

The invariants — what a correct implementation must satisfy, and why —
live in `hcw_orbital_mechanics.livemd`, along with their derivations and
the executable assertions that check them. That document is the
specification, and it is deliberately kept free of plotting: it changes
rarely, it is reviewed as mathematics, and CI runs it as a gate.

This one is free to change as often as it likes. Nothing here gates
anything. If a plot below looks wrong, the answer is not in this file —
run the checkpoint against the specification notebook and read the
numbers.

Read the specification first if you want to know *why* these shapes are
the right shapes. What follows assumes it, and does not re-derive it.

## Scenario

A 400 km circular low Earth orbit, with a deputy offset 1 km radially —
the same scenario the specification notebook asserts against.

```elixir
alias Plumbline.Dynamics.HCW
alias VegaLite, as: Vl

# WGS-84 / EGM-96 Earth gravitational parameter, km^3/s^2.
mu = 398_600.4418

# Equatorial radius + altitude, km.
a = 6378.137 + 400.0

# Mean motion (rad/s) and orbital period (s).
n = :math.sqrt(mu / (a * a * a))
period = 2 * :math.pi() / n

steps = 5_000
dt = period / steps
x0 = 1.0

%{
  mean_motion_rad_s: n,
  period_min: Float.round(period / 60, 3)
}
```

## The drift-free in-plane orbit

The drift-free in-plane orbit, in the conventional LVLH orientation:
along-track on the horizontal axis, radial on the vertical.

A correct implementation draws a closed ellipse. One that has broken the
coupling between the axes draws a spiral, and the failure is visible
without reading a single number.

```elixir
initial = %HCW.State{
  x: x0,
  y: 0.0,
  z: 0.0,
  vx: 0.0,
  vy: -2 * n * x0,
  vz: 0.0
}

points =
  initial
  |> HCW.propagate(n, dt, period)
  |> Enum.take_every(10)
  |> Enum.map(fn {t, s} ->
    %{"along_track_km" => s.y, "radial_km" => s.x, "t_min" => t / 60}
  end)

Vl.new(width: 520, height: 320, title: "Relative motion in the LVLH plane (one orbit)")
|> Vl.data_from_values(points)
|> Vl.mark(:line, point: false, stroke_width: 2)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "along_track_km",
  type: :quantitative,
  title: "Along-track y (km)",
  scale: [zero: false]
)
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "radial_km",
  type: :quantitative,
  title: "Radial x (km)",
  scale: [zero: false]
)
|> Vl.encode_field(:order, "t_min", type: :quantitative)
```

## One character, thirty-seven kilometres

The drift-free condition belongs to one velocity component. Applied to
the other, it is a transposed subscript — one character, and it passes
code review. The specification derives which component, and what the
transposition costs per orbit.

Both trajectories below come from the same `propagate/4`. Only the
initial state differs.

```elixir
transposed = %HCW.State{
  x: x0,
  y: 0.0,
  z: 0.0,
  vx: -2 * n * x0,
  vy: 0.0,
  vz: 0.0
}

both_cases = fn span ->
  for {state, label} <- [{initial, "drift-free (vy₀)"}, {transposed, "transposed (vx₀)"}],
      {t, s} <- state |> HCW.propagate(n, dt, span) |> Enum.take_every(5) do
    %{"along_track_km" => s.y, "radial_km" => s.x, "t_min" => t / 60, "case" => label}
  end
end

compare = fn points, title ->
  Vl.new(width: 520, height: 320, title: title)
  |> Vl.data_from_values(points)
  |> Vl.mark(:line, point: false, stroke_width: 2)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:x, "along_track_km",
    type: :quantitative,
    title: "Along-track y (km)",
    scale: [zero: false]
  )
  |> Vl.encode_field(:y, "radial_km",
    type: :quantitative,
    title: "Radial x (km)",
    scale: [zero: false]
  )
  |> Vl.encode_field(:color, "case", type: :nominal, title: nil)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:order, "t_min", type: :quantitative)
end

compare.(both_cases.(600.0), "First ten minutes — the two are indistinguishable")
```

That is the entire problem. Ten minutes in, on a plot, by eye, in a
short-window unit test, the wrong trajectory is the right trajectory.

The same two initial states over one full orbit. Watch the horizontal
axis: the correct orbit stays bounded, so at this scale it collapses to
a sliver near zero while the other walks off the plot and does not come
back.

```elixir
compare.(both_cases.(period), "One orbit later")
```

## The circular relative orbit

Adding cross-track motion, in phase with the radial motion, turns the
ellipse above into a genuine circle lying in a plane tilted out of the
orbital plane. The specification's bonus section gives the condition and
the resulting radius.

Projected onto plain `(x, y)` it would look like the *same* ellipse as
above, since `z` appears in neither axis. The plot below therefore uses
a combination of `x` and `z` chosen so the horizontal axis lies in the
orbit's own plane. Paired with `y(t)`, that shows the orbit as it
actually looks *within the plane it occupies* — an exact view, not an
illustrative approximation.

```elixir
circular = %HCW.State{
  x: x0,
  y: 0.0,
  z: :math.sqrt(3) * x0,
  vx: 0.0,
  vy: -2 * n * x0,
  vz: 0.0
}

circular_points =
  circular
  |> HCW.propagate(n, dt, period)
  |> Enum.take_every(10)
  |> Enum.map(fn {t, s} ->
    %{
      "tilted_radial_km" => 0.5 * s.x + :math.sqrt(3) / 2 * s.z,
      "along_track_km" => s.y,
      "t_min" => t / 60
    }
  end)

Vl.new(
  width: 340,
  height: 340,
  title: "Circular relative orbit, viewed in its own tilted plane"
)
|> Vl.data_from_values(circular_points)
|> Vl.mark(:line, point: false, stroke_width: 2)
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "tilted_radial_km",
  type: :quantitative,
  title: "In-plane radial (within the orbit's own plane, km)",
  scale: [zero: false]
)
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "along_track_km",
  type: :quantitative,
  title: "Along-track y (km)",
  scale: [zero: false]
)
|> Vl.encode_field(:order, "t_min", type: :quantitative)
```
